# 5-page report text and tables.
pages=[]
pages.append([('title',TITLE),('sub','Integrated research report  |  evidence through Phase 5E'),('h','Abstract'),('p','Quantum error-correction systems use repeated measurements to detect faults before they corrupt protected information. A decoder maps these patterns to a likely correction. Most systems use a fixed noise model, although real devices can drift. Q-SHIFT investigates whether recent detector data can guide a choice among decoder models. We developed a Python prototype using Stim circuit simulation, PyMatching matching decoders, a nine-model gate/measurement grid, detector-window estimation, and a causal controller with a pooled-fixed fallback. Condition-specific calibration sometimes improved on a nominal fixed model under measurement-heavy noise. However, Phase 4C compared calibration with a stronger pooled fixed decoder across three regimes and found no overall gain: the equal-regime difference was +0.00001556, its 95% interval crossed zero, and p=0.667. Later adaptive testing remained exploratory; the latest Phase 5E runner did not switch away from its fallback. Q-SHIFT is a working research prototype, not evidence of hardware deployment or reliable adaptive superiority.'),('h','1. Introduction and real-world problem'),('p','Quantum computers encode information in qubits, which are sensitive to imperfect gates, measurements, and environmental disturbance. Error correction adds redundancy and repeatedly measures checks on groups of qubits. The outcomes, called syndrome or detector data, do not directly identify each fault; a decoder uses their pattern to estimate a correction. When the assumed noise model differs from the current device, a fixed decoder may make less effective choices. Surface codes are studied because they use local checks on a two-dimensional layout [3].'),('p','This project asks whether a decoder can respond to changing mixtures of faults using only measured detector information. The motivation is long quantum computation, where conditions may drift during execution. This is a potential application, not a demonstrated device result.'),('h','2. Objective and hypothesis'),('p','The objective is to build and evaluate a detector-informed system that chooses among matching decoders as noise changes. The hypothesis is that a causal estimator can identify a useful change early enough for a specialized decoder to lower residual logical errors relative to a strong fixed baseline. Classifying noise is not enough: candidate decoders must produce different corrections that reliably reduce errors.')])
pages.append([('h','3. Q-SHIFT system architecture'),('arch',None),('cap','Figure 1. Completed detector blocks inform a decoder choice for a later block. Labels are used only for offline scoring. The hardware interface shown is proposed, not implemented.'),('p','Stim generates a rotated surface-code Z-memory circuit and samples gate and measurement faults [1]. It records detector outcomes and one logical observable. PyMatching builds a matching decoder from a Stim detector error model (DEM), a graph-like summary of how faults create detection events [2]. The decoder bank contains nine gate/measurement probability pairs. A pooled fixed decoder remains the fallback.'),('h','4. Implementation and experimental method'),('p','The main surface-code studies use distance 3 and five measurement rounds. Native Stim circuit-level depolarizing gate noise and basis-appropriate measurement flips are varied across data-heavy (gate 0.006, measurement 0.001), nominal (0.003, 0.003), and measurement-heavy (0.001, 0.006) regimes. These are probabilities per channel invocation, not equal total circuit-fault budgets. Circuit structure and detector/observable definitions remain consistent.'),('p','Calibration selects among nine DEM models using detector data only. The Phase 4 method smooths template detector marginals, then scores calibration counts with a composite Bernoulli likelihood and deterministic grid-order tie-breaking. Logical labels are reserved for validation and test scoring. A residual logical error occurs when the sampled observable flip differs from the predicted correction. Paired comparisons give all decoders identical detector shots and labels.'),('p','For dynamic development, an estimator compares recent completed detector windows with stored profiles. The controller includes confidence, hysteresis, dwell-time, switch-penalty, and fixed-fallback options. It chooses for the next block using earlier detector data. Hidden-regime and hindsight comparisons are privileged diagnostics, not deployable policies. No confirmatory adaptive test has run.')])
chron=[('Starter audit','Legacy eight-bit detector set','150,000 rows had only 61 unique patterns in the audit. Repeated rows undermine random splits; early classifier/XOR metrics are not validated decoder evidence.'),('Phase 1','Repetition-code regimes and recognition','Recognition was reported at 94.4%, but guarded adaptation made zero switches and tied fixed matching. Per-regime report/JSON totals conflict.'),('Phase 2','Composition-specific repetition-code matchers','Ten seeds, six conditions; validation selected fixed matching in all 60 choices. No specialist gate passed.'),('Phase 3A / 3B','Surface-code pilot and replication','Pilot signal failed multiplicity control; independent 3B test found 43,675 specialist vs 43,673 fixed errors.'),('Phase 4A / 4B','Circuit-level gate and measurement noise','Static calibration improved measurement-heavy results vs nominal fixed. 4B generated fresh templates contrary to written reuse protocol.'),('Phase 4C','Baseline selection and held-out test','1.2M development shots froze baselines; 1.8M Stage 2 shots found no overall calibrated gain vs pooled fixed.'),('Phase 5 v3','Seven dynamic development schedules','61,440 paired shots; utility and ML policies were worse in aggregate than pooled fixed.'),('Phase 5E audit','Decoder specialization feasibility','Nine graph signatures differ. Executed source/JSON: four families x three repeats; adaptive made zero switches.')]
pages.append([('h','5. Chronology of completed research'),('chron',chron),('h','What the process changed'),('p','The project moved from a repetitive eight-bit repetition-code dataset toward controlled surface-code circuits with explicit detector and logical-observable definitions. It replaced accuracy-focused classification with residual logical-error comparisons, paired shot-level differences, and seed-level uncertainty. Baselines also became stronger: nominal matching is informative, but a pooled decoder selected on development data is a harder test.'),('p','Phase 3A v1 used an invalid probability interpretation and is excluded. Corrected v2-v4 reused the same seed bundles and form one pilot, not three independent replications. Phase 3B is the fresh replication. Phase 4C Stage 1 selected baseline models on development samples before Stage 2 testing.')])
resrows=[['Study','Test design','Errors / key statistic','Interpretation'],['Phase 3B specialist vs fixed','600k paired; 20 roots','43,675 vs 43,673; p=.9374','No replicated gain.'],['Phase 4A calibrated vs nominal','600k measurement-heavy','1,118 vs 940; discordances 80/258; p=5.60e-23','Static condition-specific gain.'],['Phase 4B calibrated vs nominal','600k; 20 roots','1,165 vs 1,008; discordances 99/256; p=3.73e-17','Repeat; fresh-template deviation.'],['Phase 4C calibrated vs pooled','1.8M; 60 roots','12,435 vs 12,407; difference=+.00001556; CI [-.00004889,+.00007667]; p=.6666','No overall gain.']]
pages.append([('h','6. Verified experimental results'),('chart',None),('cap','Figure 2. Residual error rates. Phase 4A/4B compare with nominal fixed; Stage 2 uses stronger pooled fixed. The Stage 2 interval appears in the table.'),('results',resrows),('p','Stage 2 calibrated-minus-pooled errors were +77 data-heavy, +47 nominal, and -96 measurement-heavy. The net +28 errors over three equal-sized regimes gives the near-zero primary difference. Its 100,000-draw paired root-cluster sign-flip test gave p=.66658; the regime-stratified seed-bootstrap 95% interval included zero. This is not evidence of an overall gain.'),('p','Phases 4A and 4B support a narrower finding: calibrated static models reduced errors relative to a nominal model in measurement-heavy noise. They do not test online switching and do not establish superiority over pooled fixed. Phase 4B predictions were audited, but template generation departed from protocol.')])
pages.append([('h','7. Adaptive development and decoder specialization'),('p','Phase 5 v3 evaluated seven schedules: stationary, two transition directions, a return-to-nominal sequence, slow, rapid, and random changes. Across 61,440 development shots, utility selection made 435 errors versus 425 pooled; maximum-likelihood selection made 441. Results varied by schedule. These small development streams are not independent tests. Source and tests implement causal ordering, hysteresis, and fallback, but do not demonstrate a gain.'),('p','The Phase 5E saved JSON and runner show 30,000 disagreement shots per regime (90,000 total), 45,000 calibration shots, 90,000 stationary evaluation shots, and 36,864 dynamic shots: four families, three repeats, twelve blocks, 256 shots per block. Across twelve streams, both adaptive-labelled choices stayed at pooled decoder #1, made zero switches, and tied pooled errors exactly (298 each). The utility-labelled choice is the same code path as the ML choice in this runner.'),('p','The Phase 5E log instead says seven families, four repetitions, and 90,000 disagreement shots per regime. Those statements conflict with source loops and JSON and are not used. Regime-matched and calibrated-regime comparators use hidden state; hindsight uses current-block labels. These are privileged diagnostics. Nine DEM/weight signatures differ, but four model pairs had no disagreements in each sampled 30,000-shot regime.'),('h','8. Achievements and contribution'),('p','The project delivered a Stim simulation, a nine-model PyMatching bank, detector-only calibration, causal estimator/controller components, paired evaluation scripts, seed/checkpoint records, and independent audits of selected test predictions. It learned to distinguish classifier accuracy from residual logical-error reduction and to separate development, validation, and test evidence. Models with different weights can produce the same correction; different corrections do not guarantee a practical improvement. This is a useful feasibility finding, not a negative verdict on all adaptive decoding.')])
pages[-1].extend([('h','9. Proposed real-world implementation'),('p','A hardware pilot could receive syndrome outcomes from each correction cycle, maintain a rolling window, and select among prebuilt decoders for later cycles. A validated fixed decoder would remain the fallback; switching would require sustained evidence and be logged. A correction could be returned as a Pauli-frame update or an equivalent instruction. This interface is proposed only.'),('p','Deployment would require real-device readout, device-specific calibration, validation on held-out times and qubits, drift and unfamiliar-noise safeguards, and a processor-adjacent CPU/FPGA/accelerator implementation. It also needs deterministic latency guarantees, safe fallback behavior, reliable data routing, and end-to-end timing measurements. A hardware trial must compare fixed and adaptive methods on matched runs and count calibration overhead.'),('h','10. Potential applications and impact'),('p','If later proven reliable, adaptation could help protect logical information during longer computations. Better error correction may support more dependable quantum simulation in chemistry or materials research and reduce wasted processor time. These are downstream possibilities, not project results.'),('h','11. Limitations and future work'),('p','Evidence covers one small distance-3 Z-memory circuit, selected Pauli-noise models, and simulation. It does not cover hardware drift, leakage, correlated/burst faults, larger code distances, or live control. Some early tables conflict; disputed Phase 1 totals and the Phase 2 one-count oracle aggregate are not used as exact claims. Phase 4B has a template deviation. Stage 2 predictions were independently re-decoded, but calibration samples were not retained, so fitting cannot be regenerated. Phase 5E narrative counts conflict with runner/JSON. Future work should preserve calibration arrays, version analysis, use multiple independent schedule seeds, and freeze a policy before fresh held-out dynamic testing.'),('h','12. Conclusion'),('p','Q-SHIFT is a functioning simulation research prototype combining noise estimation, multiple matching decoders, and causal selection. Experiments show condition-specific static gains against a nominal model, but no reliable adaptive advantage against a strong pooled baseline. Its contribution is a transparent platform for testing when adaptation may help. Hardware benefit remains open.'),('h','References'),('refs','[1] C. Gidney, "Stim: a fast stabilizer circuit simulator," Quantum 5, 497 (2021). DOI 10.22331/Q-2021-07-06-497.\n[2] O. Higgott, "PyMatching: A Python Package for Decoding Quantum Codes with Minimum-Weight Perfect Matching," ACM Transactions on Quantum Computing 3(3), Article 16 (2022). DOI 10.1145/3505637.\n[3] A. G. Fowler, M. Mariantoni, J. M. Martinis, and A. N. Cleland, "Surface codes: Towards practical large-scale quantum computation," Physical Review A 86, 032324 (2012). DOI 10.1103/PhysRevA.86.032324.')])
# Evidence summary.
(O/'Q_SHIFT_RESULTS_AND_ACHIEVEMENTS.md').write_text('''# Q-SHIFT Results and Achievements\n\n## Overall finding\n\nThe project built a reproducible simulation prototype for comparing fixed, condition-calibrated, and detector-adaptive decoding. Adaptive superiority is not established. Calibration helped versus nominal fixed matching in a measurement-heavy condition, but Phase 4C Stage 2 found no equal-regime advantage against pooled fixed.\n\n## Verified phase record\n\n| Phase | Evidence and result | Interpretation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Starter audit | 150,000 legacy rows, 8 detector bits, 61 unique patterns | Repeated rows undermine split validity; conversation-only classifier results are not decoder evidence. |\n| Phase 1 | Reported recognition 94.4%; guarded adaptation made zero switches and tied fixed | Recognition alone did not improve decoding. Report and JSON disagree on per-regime totals. |\n| Phase 2 | 10 seeds, 6 conditions, fixed and specialist PyMatching | Validation chose fixed in all 60 seed/condition choices; no specialist passed its gate. |\n| Phase 3A | 36,000 corrected pilot shots; X specialist 2,520 vs fixed 2,528 | Unadjusted p=.0386; multiplicity-adjusted p≈.463. v2-v4 reuse pilot seeds; v1 is excluded. |\n| Phase 3B | 20 roots, 600,000 X-biased paired shots | Fixed 43,673 vs specialist 43,675; McNemar p=.9374. Pilot gain not replicated. |\n| Phase 4A | 600,000 measurement-heavy shots | Fixed 1,118 vs calibrated 940; discordances 80/258; p=5.600789e-23. Static only. |\n| Phase 4B | 20 roots, 600,000 measurement-heavy shots | Fixed 1,165 vs calibrated 1,008; discordances 99/256; p=3.73072e-17. Audit PASS WITH DEVIATION: fresh templates used instead of planned reuse. |\n| Phase 4C Stage 2 | 60 roots, 1,800,000 paired held-out shots | Calibrated 12,435 vs pooled 12,407; equal-regime difference=+0.00001556; seed-bootstrap 95% CI [-0.00004889,+0.00007667]; sign-flip p=.66658. |\n| Phase 5 v3 | 7 exploratory schedules, 61,440 paired shots | Utility adaptive 435 vs pooled 425; ML adaptive 441 vs pooled 425. Development only. |\n| Phase 5E executed audit | 4 schedule families x 3 repeats x 12 x 256 = 36,864 dynamic shots | Adaptive-labelled paths stayed at pooled decoder #1, zero switches, 298 errors each, tied with pooled. |\n\n## Phase 5E count reconciliation\n\nExecuted runner and JSON agree on 4 dynamic families x 3 repetitions. Pairwise disagreement sampling was 6 x 5,000 per regime (30,000 per regime; 90,000 total). Calibration used 45,000 shots; stationary evaluation used 90,000; dynamic evaluation used 36,864. The experiment log instead says 7 families, 4 repeats, and 90,000 pairwise shots per regime. Those numbers conflict with source/JSON and are not used. In the Phase 5E runner, `adaptive_utility` shares the ML choice path. Hidden-regime and hindsight diagnostics use unavailable test information and are not deployable methods.\n\n## Achievements\n\n- Stim surface-code simulation with detector and logical-observable output; nine-model PyMatching bank.\n- Detector-only model calibration and a causal estimator/controller with pooled-fixed fallback.\n- Paired evaluation, seed-level uncertainty, frozen baseline selection, reproducibility manifests, and independent audits of saved predictions.\n- Evidence that noise recognition and decoder specialization are distinct: model weights can differ without changing corrections, and changed corrections do not guarantee improvement.\n- Negative findings, protocol deviations, and reporting defects preserved rather than removed.\n\n## Evidence boundary\n\nAll results are simulated. Phase 4C Stage 2 predictions were independently re-decoded, but calibration samples were not saved, so fitted choices cannot be fully reconstructed. There is no hardware integration, latency test, or confirmatory adaptive test.\n''',encoding='utf-8')
(O/'Q_SHIFT_REPRODUCIBILITY.md').write_text('''# Q-SHIFT Reproducibility Guide\n\n## Scope\n\nThis is an evidence-synthesis and documentation pass. No simulations were run and historical data were not changed. Phase reports, runner source, structured results, previous audits, and the 208-file handoff inventory were consulted. This package does not claim a new replay of every historic shot array.\n\n## Artifact map\n\n- `outputs/phase4a_model_adequacy/`: five-round circuit, calibration, validation/test runner, results, audit.\n- `outputs/phase4b_replication/`: independent measurement-heavy repeat and raw test checkpoints. Fresh templates were used despite a written reuse protocol.\n- `outputs/phase4c_strong_baselines/`: Stage 1 frozen baseline selection; `stage2/` contains plan, bundles, recovery analysis, and results.\n- `outputs/phase4c_strong_baselines/adaptive/`: controller, decoder bank, evaluation, schedules, tests, v2/v3 and Phase 5D/5E evidence.\n- `outputs/master_project_handoff/`: file inventory, experiment registry, consolidated report, negative findings, and methodology notes.\n\n## Existing reproduction commands (not run for this package)\n\nRun only after reading the matching frozen config and use a new output location; do not write into historical result folders.\n\n```powershell\npython outputs/phase4a_model_adequacy/run_phase4a.py\npython outputs/phase4b_replication/run_phase4b.py\npython outputs/phase4c_strong_baselines/run_stage1.py\npython outputs/phase4c_strong_baselines/adaptive/run_phase5e.py\n```\n\nStage 2 `stage2/analyze_saved_checkpoints.py` analyzes saved test bundles. It cannot recreate calibration arrays that were not stored.\n\n## Method and software\n\nThe primary circuit is Stim `surface_code:rotated_memory_z`, distance 3, five rounds. Gate/measurement probability candidates are the 3x3 grid over 0.001, 0.003, and 0.006. Stim samples detector data and one logical observable; PyMatching decodes from decomposed DEMs. Detector-only fitting uses smoothed template marginal profiles, a composite Bernoulli score, and deterministic grid-order tie-breaking. Labels are reserved for validation/test residual scoring. Residual error is sampled observable XOR predicted correction. Paired comparisons share identical detector shots.\n\nPhase 5E reports Python 3.12.10, Stim 1.16.0, PyMatching 2.4.0, NumPy 2.5.3. Stage 2 primary analysis uses equal regime weighting, 100,000 paired root-cluster sign-flip assignments (seed 74000001), and 100,000 regime-stratified bootstrap draws for its 95% interval.\n\n## Unresolved gaps and inconsistencies\n\n- Stage 2 has 60 roots and 1.8M held-out shots; saved predictions and counts were independently checked. Calibration arrays are absent, so calibration selection cannot be regenerated end-to-end.\n- Phase 4B fresh-template generation deviated from its frozen reuse protocol.\n- Phase 1 report/JSON per-regime totals disagree. Phase 2 report prose says 2,402 noise-informed errors while table and JSON sum to 2,401; disputed totals are not headline claims.\n- Phase 3A corrected revisions share seed bundles; Phase 3B is the independent replication.\n- Phase 5E prose counts disagree with executable source/JSON; this package uses 4x3 dynamic accounting.\n- There are no hardware results, integration measurements, or end-to-end latency data.\n\n## Verified references\n\n1. C. Gidney, "Stim: a fast stabilizer circuit simulator," *Quantum* 5, 497 (2021). DOI 10.22331/Q-2021-07-06-497. https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.02202\n2. O. Higgott, "PyMatching: A Python Package for Decoding Quantum Codes with Minimum-Weight Perfect Matching," *ACM Transactions on Quantum Computing* 3(3), Article 16 (2022). DOI 10.1145/3505637. https://doi.org/10.1145/3505637\n3. A. G. Fowler, M. Mariantoni, J. M. Martinis, and A. N. Cleland, "Surface codes: Towards practical large-scale quantum computation," *Physical Review A* 86, 032324 (2012). DOI 10.1103/PhysRevA.86.032324. https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.0928\n''',encoding='utf-8')
def add_table_doc(doc,rows,widths,size=7.2):
 t=doc.add_table(rows=0,cols=len(widths));t.alignment=WD_TABLE_ALIGNMENT.CENTER;t.autofit=False
 for ri,row in enumerate(rows):
  cells=t.add_row().cells
  for j,val in enumerate(row):
   cells[j].width=Inches(widths[j]);cells[j].vertical_alignment=WD_CELL_VERTICAL_ALIGNMENT.TOP;p=cells[j].paragraphs[0];p.paragraph_format.space_after=Pt(1);r=p.add_run(str(val));r.font.size=Pt(size);r.font.color.rgb=RGBColor(39,55,71)
   if ri==0:r.bold=True;r.font.color.rgb=RGBColor(255,255,255);shade(cells[j],'183756')
   else:shade(cells[j],'F2F6F8' if ri%2 else 'E8F0F4')
   border(cells[j],'FFFFFF')
 return t
